Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that

$$
\begin{aligned}T(kv+w)=T(kv)+T(w)=kT(v)+T(w)\end{aligned}
$$

for any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}T(2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) & =2T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2}) \\ [\begin{aligned}11 \\ 3\end{aligned}] & =2T({v}_{1})+[\begin{aligned}5 \\ -19\end{aligned}] \\ 2T({v}_{1}) & =[\begin{aligned}11 \\ 3\end{aligned}]-[\begin{aligned}5 \\ -19\end{aligned}] \\ 2T({v}_{1}) & =[\begin{aligned}6 \\ 22\end{aligned}] \\ T({v}_{1}) & =\frac{1}{2}[\begin{aligned}6 \\ 22\end{aligned}] \\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\begin{aligned}3 \\ 11\end{aligned}].\end{aligned}
$$

Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that

$$
\begin{aligned}T(v+kw)=T(v)+T(kw)=T(v)+kT(w)\end{aligned}
$$

for any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}+3{v}_{2}) & =T({v}_{1})+3T({v}_{2}) \\ [\begin{aligned}15 \\ 6\end{aligned}] & =[\begin{aligned}6 \\ -12\end{aligned}]+3T({v}_{2}) \\ 3T({v}_{2}) & =[\begin{aligned}15 \\ 6\end{aligned}]-[\begin{aligned}6 \\ -12\end{aligned}] \\ 3T({v}_{2}) & =[\begin{aligned}9 \\ 18\end{aligned}] \\ T({v}_{2}) & =\frac{1}{3}[\begin{aligned}9 \\ 18\end{aligned}] \\ T({v}_{2}) & =[\begin{aligned}3 \\ 6\end{aligned}].\end{aligned}
$$

Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that

$$
\begin{aligned}T(kv+hw)=T(kv)+T(hw)=kT(v)+hT(w)\end{aligned}
$$

for any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}T(4{v}_{1}-5{v}_{2}) & =4T({v}_{1})-5T({v}_{2}) \\ [\begin{aligned}-13 \\ 6\end{aligned}] & =4[\begin{aligned}-2 \\ 4\end{aligned}]-5T({v}_{2}) \\ 5T({v}_{2}) & =4[\begin{aligned}-2 \\ 4\end{aligned}]-[\begin{aligned}-13 \\ 6\end{aligned}] \\ 5T({v}_{2}) & =[\begin{aligned}-8 \\ 16\end{aligned}]-[\begin{aligned}-13 \\ 6\end{aligned}] \\ 5T({v}_{2}) & =[\begin{aligned}5 \\ 10\end{aligned}] \\ T({v}_{2}) & =\frac{1}{5}[\begin{aligned}5 \\ 10\end{aligned}] \\ T({v}_{2}) & =[\begin{aligned}1 \\ 2\end{aligned}].\end{aligned}
$$
